#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""vpn-bot-cert — the bot's TLS certificate: issued by acme.sh, laid out by the bot (docs/certificate.md).

WHY. acme.sh keeps ONE install-cert per domain: the paths it copies to and ONE reloadcmd.
A move of the host once left that reloadcmd naming only a part of the readers, and the
others served an expiring certificate. The readers are not configuration on the host any
more: they are THE REGISTRY below (code, reviewed, tested), and the reloadcmd acme.sh
calls after every renewal is this helper:

    acme.sh --install-cert -d <domain> [--ecc] --reloadcmd "/usr/local/sbin/vpn-bot-cert deploy"

with no --cert-file / --key-file / --fullchain-file at all: acme.sh writes nothing outside
its own directory, `deploy` lays the material out — atomically, consumer by consumer.

THE REGISTRY (CONSUMERS). Each consumer: the files it reads (path, which half of the pair,
owner:group, mode), the unit and what happens to it after an update. A consumer is ACTIVE
only while its module is installed here: its unit exists (`systemctl cat`), it was not
removed from the host (/etc/vpn-bot/uninstalled/<marker>, vpn-bot-protocol-uninstall) and
its directory is there (or the registry creates it).

  hysteria      /etc/hysteria/cert.pem hysteria:hysteria 0644 (fullchain)
                /etc/hysteria/key.pem  hysteria:hysteria 0600            restart hysteria-server
  nginx         /etc/vpn-bot/tls/fullchain.pem root:vpn-bot 0640
                /etc/vpn-bot/tls/privkey.pem   root:vpn-bot 0640         nginx -t, reload nginx
  subscription  the same two files                                       try-restart vpn-bot-subscription

  (/etc/vpn-bot/tls itself root:root 0755.) These are exactly the ownerships the tracked
  hook deploy/vpn-bot-acme-reload.sh re-asserts — measured on the main server.

VERBS

  status [--json]
      Read-only. Every acme.sh domain (SAN, expiry, key, reloadcmd, the Cloudflare token it
      renews with — a fingerprint only), the bot's domain (the one whose reloadcmd is
      `vpn-bot-cert deploy`) or the domain to adopt, every consumer (installed or why not,
      its files: present, sha equal to acme.sh's, owner and mode, what it actually reads),
      the bot's token (fingerprint) and the list of problems with a level. The self-check
      and the bot's «Сертификат и DNS» read this.
  deploy [--domain D] [--all] [--dry-run] [--json]
      THE reloadcmd. The source is acme.sh's own pair of the domain (Le_Domain and the
      working directory acme.sh gives, else the adopted domain): fullchain.cer +
      <domain>.key, checked first (the key matches the certificate, the certificate has
      not expired, its SAN covers the domain's names). Consumers sharing a file are one
      GROUP. Per group: a changed file is written NEXT to its target (same directory,
      owner and mode set), the staged pair is checked again with openssl, the old copy is
      kept, then the new files are renamed over the old ones; then the actions. A failed
      action (or a unit not active after its restart) puts the old files back for THAT
      group and runs its actions again on them — the other groups keep the new pair.
      A consumer is acted on only when its files changed or the state file says it last
      took another pair (--all: every active consumer). Exit 1 when a group was rolled
      back. When acme.sh runs it from inside `issue` / `renew` / `adopt` (env
      VPN_BOT_CERT_NESTED) it defers to that verb, which deploys itself afterwards.
  issue --names a[,b...] --zone ZONE [--json]
      acme.sh DNS-01 with Cloudflare (dns_cf) and the BOT's token
      (/etc/vpn-bot/dns_failover/cloudflare_token — the one file of the bot; acme.sh gets it
      as CF_Token in its environment, never on a command line) for ZONE — the zone NAME
      the bot checked the token against; its id is asked from Cloudflare with the same
      token (GET /zones?name=ZONE) and handed to acme.sh as CF_Zone_ID, so a zone id
      another setup left in acme.sh's account.conf is never used. Let's Encrypt, the key type the domain already has (ec-256 for a
      new one), --force. acme.sh itself is installed first when it is missing (a pinned
      release, sha256-checked). Then install-cert as above, then deploy. The certificate's
      name (acme.sh's main domain) cannot change here: that is a domain change.
  renew --force [--zone ZONE] [--json]
      `acme.sh --renew --force` of the bot's domain — to prove the renewal works end to
      end. With the bot's token, the domain's saved CF_Token / CF_Zone_ID are set to it
      first (acme.sh sources the domain's conf on a renewal), so cron renews with the same
      token from then on. Then deploy.
  adopt [--domain D] [--dry-run] [--json]
      The migration of a host set up by hand: the existing acme.sh domain is taken AS IS —
      the certificate is not reissued. Only its install-cert is rewritten (`--install-cert`
      with the reloadcmd above and no file targets), then deploy (changed only: a consumer
      whose copy already equals acme.sh's is not restarted). --dry-run prints the change.
  act [--json]
      Every installed reader's check and action on the files it has now — nothing is
      written. The wizard's rollback runs it after it restored a backup, so every reader
      runs the restored pair again.

acme.sh takes no lock, and its cron does not take this helper's. While the helper's own
acme.sh works on a domain (issue / renew / adopt) that domain's Le_NextRenewTime is pushed an
hour ahead — `acme.sh --cron` skips a domain that is not due — and put back afterwards unless
acme.sh wrote a new one; THEN the helper waits (up to 5 minutes, then refuses) for an acme.sh
already running. A cron renewal's own `deploy` waits for this helper's lock.

Exit codes: 0 ok; 1 a consumer failed and its group was rolled back; 2 usage; 3 refused
(nothing to adopt, no token, a bad pair, another domain); 5 cannot (a tool or a file).

Stdlib only, every command an argv list (never a shell), the token only ever in a child's
environment. Run as root from another mount namespace (the non-root bot's
ProtectSystem=strict, kept by sudo) it re-executes itself in PID 1's first (nsenter).
TEST SEAM: VPN_BOT_CERT_SELFTEST=1 needs --root (every host path under it), resolves every
command inside VPN_BOT_CERT_SELFTEST_BIN (or an absolute path under --root) and keeps
owners in a sidecar file instead of chown: deploy.sh runs the test suite as root on the
production host.
"""
